Using Charles’s Law, temperatures must be in kelvin:

\[
T_1 = 137 + 273 = 410\ \text{K}
\]

\[
T_2 = T_1\frac{V_2}{V_1}
= 410\frac{425}{250}
= 697\ \text{K}
\]

Converting back to Celsius:

\[
T_2 = 697 - 273 = 424^\circ\text{C}
\]

The assistant incorrectly converted \(137^\circ\text{C}\) to \(400\ \text{K}\) instead of \(410\ \text{K}\), leading to the wrong final answer of \(407^\circ\text{C}\). The reference answer of \(424^\circ\text{C}\) is correct, so the benchmark’s WRONG label is justified.

